Telephony: probe radio HAL versions without blocking on missing ones

Android 11's RILJ walks the radio HAL versions from 1.5 down to 1.0, calling
`IRadio.getService(name, retry=true)` for each.  With retry=true the HIDL
client keeps the call pending forever for an interface that is not registered,
so on a device whose vendor RIL only provides 1.1 (our stock 6.0.1
libsec-ril.so through AOSP's libril) com.android.phone hangs in the 1.2 probe
and never talks to the RIL at all: no baseband version, no SIM state, no
calls, and no RIL requests reach the modem.

Probe with retry=false instead.  The daemon registers 1.0/1.1 at boot and the
probe then falls through to the version that actually exists; a lazy HAL that
has not started yet is still handled by the retry inside hwservicemanager's
ctl.interface_start path, so real devices keep their previous behaviour.

Applied to frameworks/opt/telephony (git apply -p1 from that directory).

diff --git a/src/java/com/android/internal/telephony/RIL.java b/src/java/com/android/internal/telephony/RIL.java
index 3ef440f13..d0ae2af3a 100644
--- a/src/java/com/android/internal/telephony/RIL.java
+++ b/src/java/com/android/internal/telephony/RIL.java
@@ -491,7 +491,7 @@ public class RIL extends BaseCommands implements CommandsInterface {
             } else {
                 try {
                     mRadioProxy = android.hardware.radio.V1_5.IRadio.getService(
-                            HIDL_SERVICE_NAME[mPhoneId], true);
+                            HIDL_SERVICE_NAME[mPhoneId], false);
                     mRadioVersion = RADIO_HAL_VERSION_1_5;
                 } catch (NoSuchElementException e) {
                 }
@@ -499,7 +499,7 @@ public class RIL extends BaseCommands implements CommandsInterface {
                 if (mRadioProxy == null) {
